= Judgment under Uncertainty: Heuristics and Biases / 不确定性下的判断：启发式与偏差

#html.p(id: "en-G001")[#text("The article examines three shortcuts in uncertain judgment: representativeness, availability, and adjustment from an anchor. Each can save effort while introducing predictable errors.")]

#zh(id: "G001")[#text("文章考察不确定性判断中的三种捷径：代表性、可得性，以及从锚点出发的调整。它们能节省思考，也会带来可预测的错误。")]

#html.p(id: "en-G002")[#text("A description may seem representative of a category; an example may come readily to mind; a suggested number may shape an estimate. These cues are not interchangeable with probability evidence.")]

#zh(id: "G002")[#text("一段描述可能很像某个类别，一个例子可能很容易被想起，一个给出的数字可能影响估计。这些线索并不等同于概率证据。")]

#html.p(id: "en-G003")[#text("Try making your own judgment before reading an experiment's discussion. Then identify the information you used and the information you neglected. The original conditions matter.")]

#zh(id: "G003")[#text("可以先作出自己的判断，再读实验讨论。接着找出自己使用了哪些信息，又忽略了哪些信息。原实验的条件很重要。")]

#html.p(id: "en-G004")[#text("Reading question: when does a useful shortcut cease to track the quantity being estimated? This is more informative than simply labeling an answer irrational.")]

#zh(id: "G004")[#text("阅读时可以追问：一种通常有用的捷径，什么时候不再对应我们正在估计的量？这比简单把某个答案贴上“不理性”的标签更有帮助。")]
